 QogQUuyFUx8,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,"Live Off $500,000 In The Bank And Do Nothing Else",190887,STRONG,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Live Off $500,000 In The Bank And Do Nothing Else' is an imperative lifestyle promise built on one concrete, round, reachable-sounding dollar figure ($500,000), a deliberately low-tech location ('In The Bank', not 'invested') and an extreme-ease payoff ('Do Nothing Else'). The number undercuts the common $1M-plus retirement benchmark, so the title implies a contrarian 'you need far less than you think' answer without stating it. It is a pure promise with no question, no caveat and no 'math' tag. Same-channel comparisons show the dollar figure alone does not predict outcome: kVpJ1Elw680 'Retire on $1,000,000? The Shocking Reality' (eight days later) 0.15245x WEAK frames a bigger number as a warning/question; HDBlb-oaXeM 'The $2M-$3M Early Retirement Trap Nobody Warns You About' 0.74375x TYPICAL is also a threat frame; jpD1YXj0JRM 'Why Everything Changes After You Hit $500,000 Invested' 1.54694x TYPICAL uses the same number as a milestone rather than an exit; UfqNzF7sQzo 'Why Everything Changes After $3,000 Month Passive Income' 1.6745x TYPICAL. Income-yield titles are split: BL1eTZxWc4A 'How Much You Need to Make $1,000 Month in Dividends?' 4.15859x STRONG versus LJM5qILr3SE 'What $1,000,000 Really Pays in Dividends' 0.55686x TYPICAL and zppYzhlGiOc 'What $100K Invested Really Pays in Dividends (Realistically)' 0.46591x WEAK; uXkF1LtRZso 'How To Retire Early With One House' 0.21768x WEAK. In this sample the strongest titles promise an attainable outcome (live off, make $1,000 a month) while 'reality/realistically/trap' framings of similar money sizes are weaker; this is a descriptive pattern across nine titles, not a proven cause.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QogQUuyFUx8"", ""audit-batches/NICK_149_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate explicit intent ('live off $500,000', 'can I retire on 500k', 'how much interest on 500k') exists as a recognizable query family, but the 'do nothing else' phrasing is browse/aspiration framing. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned and partly contradicted. The script does open on the exact promise ('live comfortably with just half a million dollars in the bank') and computes income on $500,000. But 'In The Bank' is quickly replaced: the 5% case uses 'bonds, high yield savings accounts, and other low-risk options', and the main $42,500 case uses a stock/bond portfolio at 8.5%, which is not money in the bank. 'Do Nothing Else' is also undercut: more than half the script is about saving $2,000 a month for 18 years, cutting spending, and discipline, and it adds Social Security as an extra income source. The title promises an endpoint; the script mostly sells the accumulation mindset.",,"[""kVpJ1Elw680"", ""jpD1YXj0JRM"", ""HDBlb-oaXeM"", ""UfqNzF7sQzo"", ""BL1eTZxWc4A"", ""LJM5qILr3SE"", ""zppYzhlGiOc"", ""uXkF1LtRZso""]",
-QtXhRiEJ9zo,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Once You Hit $100K The Math Does The Rest,7837,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Once You Hit $100K The Math Does The Rest' is a threshold-promise title: one concrete dollar milestone ($100K), a conditional trigger (Once You Hit) and a relief payoff (The Math Does The Rest) that implies effort ends after the milestone. It has no curiosity gap beyond the familiar 'compounding takes over' idea, no named rule and no surprising number. The channel has run this exact premise many times with very different results. Stronger siblings add a counterintuitive ratio or a named mechanism: hvcr7gVbcr0 '$100,000 Means You're 1/3 Of The Way To $1,000,000 (The Surprising Math)' 2.85698x STRONG, EjzQXyyREgE 'This is Why Compounding Feels Slow (Until the 7-5-3-1 Rule Kicks In)' 3.43406x STRONG, r30ujBFB50A 'How to Activate the ""Snowball Effect"" (When Money Starts Making Money)' 2.18934x STRONG, and KrvtiC-Jaz0 'Let's Watch What Happens to People AFTER They Hit $100,000' 4.39531x STRONG (a people/story frame rather than a math frame). Siblings that restate the generic hard-first-100K idea sit TYPICAL or WEAK: BSlwkVIozNo 'Why the First $100,000 is Brutally Hard - But the Next is Surprisingly Easy' 0.56009x TYPICAL, cGVuTIDDqtc 'The First $100k is the Hardest (Why 75% Fail)' 0.72251x TYPICAL, 6IK8KJUGZMY 'At What Age Do Most People Hit $100K, $500K, & $1M?' 0.37449x WEAK and S0xwBH_fAn0 'Why $100K Doesn't Feel Rich Anymore' 0.44202x WEAK. This title (0.81903x TYPICAL) lands with the generic group. Within these eight titles, a named rule, ratio or human story co-occurs with STRONG results and a plain restatement of the premise with TYPICAL/WEAK results; this is a descriptive pattern in a small sample, and repeated exposure of the same audience to the premise is an uncontrolled confounder.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QtXhRiEJ9zo"", ""audit-batches/NICK_150_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate evergreen intent around 'first 100k', 'compound interest' and the Munger quote; the exact phrasing is not a typical query. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned. The script does deliver the promised mechanism (the contribution-versus-return crossover, the shrinking time per additional $100K, and what an untouched $100K becomes). But the title's 'does the rest' overstates the narrator's own numbers: he computes the crossover at about $170,000 at 7% (and $120,000 at 10%), so at $100K the market is still contributing less than the saver (7% of $100K = $7,000 versus $12,000 a year contributed). He also concedes later that the inflation-adjusted Munger milestone is about $211,000 and that some people stall after $100K. The script frames $100K as an 'on-ramp' rather than the point where the math takes over, which is more accurate than the title.",,"[""hvcr7gVbcr0"", ""EjzQXyyREgE"", ""KrvtiC-Jaz0"", ""r30ujBFB50A"", ""BSlwkVIozNo"", ""cGVuTIDDqtc"", ""6IK8KJUGZMY"", ""S0xwBH_fAn0""]",
-QtlJUTJ58ZU,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Do the Opposite: The Wealth Trick Rich People Use (Inversion Thinking),19992,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Do the Opposite: The Wealth Trick Rich People Use (Inversion Thinking)' is a three-part title: an imperative paradox ('Do the Opposite'), a secret-of-the-rich promise ('The Wealth Trick Rich People Use') and a named mental model in parentheses ('Inversion Thinking'). It contains no number, dollar figure, object or time marker, and 'the opposite' of what is never stated, so the curiosity gap is wide but vague. It belongs to the channel's 'named rule or mental model' family, which mostly lands in the TYPICAL middle: EwbXdhQ0qrw 'The 80/20 Rule of Money: Build Wealth by Doing Less' 0.90095x TYPICAL (published the day before), MV3oBl8OPfM 'Eat the Frog: The Wealth Rule Nobody Follows on Payday' 1.0592x TYPICAL. The one STRONG named-rule example, v-1VwbsUa94 'The Chinese Rule That Builds Wealth' 2.57622x, has a shorter, more concrete exotic hook. The 'what the rich actually do' framing performs best when it names a concrete mechanism: 7W0uvrGuOW4 'Buy, Borrow, Die: How the Rich Actually Avoid Taxes' 5.76468x STRONG, published the next day. Counterexamples on the same 'avoid mistakes' theme show the avoidance framing alone does not lift results: e06r3ZWyBVc 'Top 5 Wealth Killers You Have to Avoid' 0.82458x TYPICAL, NAg-Xx_xy8U '7 Hidden Ways Society Programs You to Stay Poor' 0.4978x WEAK, JtMc-fNjfpo 'Wealth Is Way Easier Than You Think' 0.49581x WEAK, and the investor-name title poD3PDHgAiE 'What Warren Buffett Would Do With $10,000 Today' 0.44562x WEAK. Within this sample, abstract mindset titles cluster at WEAK to low TYPICAL and concrete-mechanism titles at STRONG; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause. Note the title never mentions Munger or Buffett, although the script leans on them.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:QtlJUTJ58ZU"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:QtlJUTJ58ZU"", ""audit-batches/NICK_151_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Some explicit search intent for 'inversion thinking' as a mental-model term, but it is a niche query; 'wealth trick rich people use' is a browse/suggested phrasing. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned. 'Inversion Thinking' is delivered: the script defines it (ask how people stay poor, then do the opposite), attributes it to Jacobi and Munger, and structures the body as 11 'guaranteed ways to stay poor'. 'Do the Opposite' is only implicitly paid off: each item describes the poor-making behavior, and the opposite action is usually left for the viewer to infer. 'The Wealth Trick Rich People Use' is weakly supported: apart from Munger/Buffett anecdotes and one Bezos story, the script does not show evidence that rich people as a group use this method. The title implies one trick; the video delivers an unannounced list of 11.",,"[""EwbXdhQ0qrw"", ""MV3oBl8OPfM"", ""v-1VwbsUa94"", ""7W0uvrGuOW4"", ""e06r3ZWyBVc"", ""NAg-Xx_xy8U"", ""JtMc-fNjfpo"", ""poD3PDHgAiE""]",
-R1AcuUo916k,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Private Credit Panic - Why Investors Are Rushing For the Exits,4790,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Private Credit Panic - Why Investors Are Rushing For the Exits' is a news-explainer title: a named asset class (Private Credit), an emotional state (Panic), and a 'Why' promise with a crowd-flight image (Rushing For the Exits). Unlike most of this channel's titles it carries no dollar figure, no 'you' and no personal threshold; the viewer must already know what private credit is to feel addressed. Same-channel macro/news-warning titles cluster in low-to-mid TYPICAL: HR6wU9Hb6xk 'Wall Street Just Gave a Dire Warning (You're Not Ready)' 1.21959x TYPICAL, v7uG773-wAY 'The Beginning of the US Debt Collapse is Here' 1.11089x TYPICAL, QlPqXGV491Q 'WTF Is Happening To The Stock Market?!' 1.20385x TYPICAL, TIrIi752GSc 'The Bond Market Is Breaking - Here's What It Does to YOUR Mortgage!' 0.61547x TYPICAL, mErWx9GiVUc 'You're Not Ready For What's Coming' 0.77667x TYPICAL, 1W9dzLgpU4k 'What The Average Person Should Do Before The Market Crash' 0.54346x TYPICAL. Counterexamples: bv5qS6sR4Bs 'Are You Ready For What's Coming?' 4.79096x STRONG (vague threat title that broke out) and Dgp4_ehMo9k 'If You Don't Understand Bonds, You Don't Understand Money' 13.70542x STRONG, an evergreen explainer of a 'boring' fixed-income asset framed as a personal knowledge gap rather than as news. At 1.19071x this video sits in the same band as the other news-warning titles; the descriptive pattern is that asset-class news framing lands TYPICAL on this channel while personal-gap framing of a similar asset class has broken out once. Eight titles; not causal.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:R1AcuUo916k"", ""audit-batches/NICK_152_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate topical search interest is plausible given 2025-2026 trade and mainstream coverage of non-traded BDC redemption queues (see event block), but 'private credit panic' is a news phrase rather than an evergreen query. Historical query volume, Google Trends and traffic sources were not fetched.","Strongly aligned. 'Rushing For the Exits' is paid off with specific redemption-queue mechanics (quarterly repurchase offers, 5% cap, proration, Alvin asking for $80,000 and receiving about $40,000), and 'Panic' with the First Brands/Tricolor collapses and Dimon's 'cockroach' remark. The script is more balanced than 'Panic' implies: it gives a steel-man section (direct lending index returns, locked funds, Blackstone's gates-as-feature argument) and closes with 'Not that private credit is a fraud because mostly it is not.'",,"[""HR6wU9Hb6xk"", ""v7uG773-wAY"", ""TIrIi752GSc"", ""QlPqXGV491Q"", ""mErWx9GiVUc"", ""1W9dzLgpU4k"", ""bv5qS6sR4Bs"", ""Dgp4_ehMo9k""]",
-RUVOjfrIemY,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,Why Your Credit Score Drops Even When You Pay On Time,5344,WEAK,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'Why Your Credit Score Drops Even When You Pay On Time' is a 'Why Your X' explainer title built on a paradox: the viewer's responsible behavior (paying on time) is paired with an unexpected penalty (score drops). It names a concrete, personally owned object (your credit score) and a common frustration, so it is clearer than the channel's abstract mindset titles. It has no number, dollar figure or stakes marker (the script's strongest payoffs, the 22-point drop and the claimed $31,200 mortgage cost, are absent from the title), and it reads like a search-style FAQ rather than a surprising reveal. Same-channel comparisons: the closest topical sibling, 4eGrsL5CwBQ 'Why Everything Changes After You Get 832 Credit Score', published three days earlier, landed at 0.54383x TYPICAL, so two credit-score titles in one week both sat near the WEAK/TYPICAL boundary. Other 'Why Your [money object]' explainers are also low: J_sTOWazmho 'Why Your $1,000 Car Payment Actually Costs $4,000 (The Hidden Math)' 0.50669x TYPICAL despite a dollar figure; debt how-to r4fpD0dgQ64 'Fast Way to Pay Off $10K in Debt on a Small Salary' 0.2221x WEAK; gwm1Hr5iipU 'The 50 Year Mortgage Trap That'll Keep You in Debt Forever' 0.32481x WEAK. Counterexamples where 'Why Your' worked attach the question to a life-stage or wealth aspiration: mWhpJZrTyNU 'Why Your First Million Feels Impossible (And The Next Comes Fast)' 2.07599x STRONG and ZnI6pKMnpf4 'Why Your 40s Beat Your 20s for Building Wealth' 1.68072x TYPICAL; HEtVmp38D4M 'Invest or Pay Off Debt? The Wealthy Know the Answer' 3.64145x STRONG frames debt as a wealth decision; tynTfEi4518 'The Car Payment Trap Nobody Talks About' 24.51666x STRONG frames a debt object as a hidden trap. Within this small sample, credit/debt remediation framing co-occurs with weak results and aspiration or trap framing with stronger ones; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:RUVOjfrIemY"", ""audit-batches/NICK_153_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Plausible explicit search intent ('why did my credit score drop', 'credit score dropped even though I paid on time' are natural queries), so the title is closer to search phrasing than most channel titles. Historical query volume, search traffic share and impressions are unavailable.","Well aligned at the start: the cold open is exactly the promised scenario (bills paid in full, score falls 22 points from 738 to 716 on utilization). The script then broadens to seven traps (utilization snapshot, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old cards, paying off an installment loan, authorized-user late payments, score-version mismatch) plus report errors and a bureau business-model segment. Some sections (inquiries, report errors, multiple score versions) are not really 'drops while paying on time', so the title undersells the breadth and the Thomas mortgage-cost payoff.",,"[""4eGrsL5CwBQ"", ""J_sTOWazmho"", ""r4fpD0dgQ64"", ""gwm1Hr5iipU"", ""mWhpJZrTyNU"", ""ZnI6pKMnpf4"", ""HEtVmp38D4M"", ""tynTfEi4518""]",
-R_E6OOnF9e4,UCXzNNA2ngRpRiWvyA7eGd6w,Nick Invests,15 Low Effort Boring Tasks That Make More Than Your Job,6609,TYPICAL,2026-09-11,COMPLETE_LIMITED_EVIDENCE,ACTUAL_TITLE,"'15 Low Effort Boring Tasks That Make More Than Your Job' stacks a numbered list (15), two effort-reducing adjectives (Low Effort, Boring) and a comparative income promise (Make More Than Your Job). The comparative is the strongest and riskiest element: it is an implied earnings claim against the viewer's own salary, with no qualifier such as 'can' or 'eventually'. It has no dollar figure, which the channel's stronger income titles use. Same-channel comparisons: the closest structural sibling, 5UiyM5UGEes '10 LAZY Money Habits That Build Quiet Wealth' 0.9092x TYPICAL, and 5x_qbbMPNq0 'It's Boring, But It Will Make You Richer Than Anyone You Know' 1.40215x TYPICAL show that 'lazy/boring' framing lands in the TYPICAL band on this channel. Titles with a concrete monthly dollar threshold do better: HWU9IQB9Q-k 'Why Everything Changes After $1K Month Passive Income' 2.27303x STRONG, vdMkNfJYqLI 'The 3 Passive Income Milestones That Change Everything' 2.00183x STRONG, UfqNzF7sQzo '$3,000 Month Passive Income' 1.6745x TYPICAL, and xtdEdCdznsg 'How To Turn $10,000 Into $500,000 (The Lazy Method)' 2.18248x STRONG, which pairs 'lazy' with a dollar transformation. A list of income options without a dollar anchor, 1JynDORGRis '12 High Paying Jobs That Don't Need a Degree' 1.11497x TYPICAL, sits mid-band, and the explainer counterexample uSZ1N2ZAmDM 'Dividends 101' 0.3188x WEAK shows that the investing items in this video are not a strong standalone draw. Descriptive pattern across eight titles: dollar-anchored passive-income titles co-occur with stronger ratios than effort-adjective lists; not a proven cause.","[""VIDEO_MASTER.csv:R_E6OOnF9e4"", ""TRANSCRIPT_COVERAGE.csv:R_E6OOnF9e4"", ""audit-batches/NICK_154_READ_RECEIPTS.json""]","Moderate generic search intent around 'passive income ideas' and 'side hustles'; the exact phrase 'boring tasks that make more than your job' is not a typical query, so most demand is likely browse/suggested. Historical query volume and traffic sources unavailable.","Partially aligned. The script delivers exactly 15 numbered items, so the count is honest. 'Low effort' is contradicted by the script's own caveats: vending machines require cold calling building managers, digital downloads need '20 to 40 hours a week for the first few months', print on demand is a multi-year back catalog, car washes and laundromats break 'on schedules they set for themselves', and fewer than 5% of laundromats run without the owner. 'Make More Than Your Job' is not delivered: the narrator's own composite example, Mary, ends at 'just over $1,300 a month' (about $15,600 a year) against a $58,000 salary and $3,400 monthly take-home (about $40,800 a year, analyst calculation). Only the laundromat item ($40,000 to $100,000 net for an active owner, after a roughly $320,000 leveraged purchase) approaches a salary, and it is neither low effort nor a 'task'. Items 6 to 10 (savings, T-bills, dividend and REIT funds, real estate lending) are investments whose income scales with capital, not tasks.",,"[""5UiyM5UGEes"", ""HWU9IQB9Q-k"", ""vdMkNfJYqLI"", ""UfqNzF7sQzo"", ""1JynDORGRis"", ""5x_qbbMPNq0"", ""xtdEdCdznsg"", ""uSZ1N2ZAmDM""]",
